{"reference":"Ezekiel 38","book":"Ezekiel","chapter":38,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A question about the chapter’s setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Ezekiel 38 introduces Gog of Magog as the leader of a large coalition that attacks a restored and secure Israel, only to be defeated by divine action. The chapter therefore presents the invasion and its outcome as one unified vision. A textual question remains: Papyrus 967 places the material usually numbered Ezekiel 38–39 before chapter 37, an ancient arrangement different from the familiar Masoretic sequence.","verse_refs":["Ezekiel 38:1-23"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_ezekiel_038_1265889393a41cd1ebb34d16"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}